            <worksheet xml:id="new-practice-sols" margin="1in" bottom="1.25cm">
            <title>New Material Practice Set (with solutions)</title>
            <exercise>
    <statement>
    


    <p>
      Rank the following functions in order of which function dominates another as <m>x \rightarrow \infty</m>.
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>y = \ln x</m>
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            <m>y=0.5^{x}</m>
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            <m>y = x</m>
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            <m>y = e^{0.1x}</m>
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            <m>y = \sqrt{x}</m>
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            <m>y = x^{1000}</m>
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                <m>0.5^x \lt \ln(x) \lt \sqrt{x} \lt x \lt x^{1000} \lt e^{0.1x}</m>
            </p>
        </solution>
</exercise>

<exercise>
        <statement>

    <p>
      Rank the following functions in order of which function dominates another as <m>x \rightarrow -\infty</m>.
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>y = 0.5^{x}</m>
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            <m>y = x</m>
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            <m>y = e^{0.1x}</m>
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            <m>y = x^{1/3}</m>
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            <m>y = x^{1000}</m>
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>
</statement>
<solution>
    <p>
        <m>e^{0.1x} \lt x^{1/3} \lt x \lt x^{1000} \lt 0.5^x</m>
    </p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise>
    <statement>
    <p>
      Find the horizontal asymptote(s) of <m>\displaystyle f(x) = \frac{3e^{-x}+ x^{-2}- 2x^{2}}{x+e^{-x}+x^{4}}</m> using the method of leading behaviors.
    </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x\to \infty} f(x) = \lim_{x\to \infty} \frac{-2x^2}{x^4}=0 </m>
            </p>
            <p>
                <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x\to -\infty} f(x) = \lim_{x\to -\infty} \frac{3e^{-x}}{e^{-x}}=3 </m>
            </p>
        </solution>
</exercise>
<exercise>
<statement>
    <p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            Which limits below does l’Hospital’s rule apply? If l’Hospital’s rule applies, use it to evaluate the limit.
          </p>

          <ol>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to 1}\frac{2^{x} - 2}{3^{x} -3}</m>
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to 1}\frac{2^{x}}{3^{x}}</m>
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to 0}\frac{\ln(1-x)}{x}</m>
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to \infty}\frac{\sin(x)}{x}</m>
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to \infty}\frac{\ln(x)}{\sqrt{x}}</m>
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>
</statement>
<solution>
    <p>
        LH Rule does not apply to <m>\lim_{x \to 1} \frac{2^x}{3^x}</m> and <m>\lim_{x \to \infty} \frac{\sin(x)}{x}</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
        <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to 1}\frac{2^x-2}{3^x-3} = \lim_{x\to 1} \frac{\ln(2)2^x}{\ln(3)3^x}= \frac{2\ln(2)}{3\ln(3)}</m>
    </p>
    <p>
        <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to 0}\frac{\ln(1-x)}{x} = \lim_{x\to 0} \frac{-1}{1-x}= -1</m>
    </p>
    <p>
        <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to \infty}\frac{\ln(x)}{\sqrt{x}} = \lim_{x\to \infty} \frac{x^{-1}}{0.5x^{-1/2}}= \lim_{x\to \infty} \frac{2}{x^{1/2}}=0</m>
    </p>
</solution>

</exercise>

<exercise>
     <statement>
    <p>
      Consider the differential equation
      <me>
        k'(x) = e^{-x}+ x^{3}
      </me>
      with initial condition <m>k(0) = -1</m>.
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            Use linear approximation centered at <m>a=0</m> to approximate <m>k(0.5)</m>.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            Euler’s method was performed to approximate <m>k(2)</m>.
            The graph is shown below.
            What value of <m>\Delta x</m> is being used in the approximation? Clearly write out the computations made using Euler’s method to show how the values in the graph were obtained.
          </p>

          <p>
            <image source="euler.png">
                <shortdescription>Piecewise linear graph passing through (0,-1), (0.5,-0.5), (1,-0.134), (1.5, 0.55), (2,2.349).</shortdescription>
            </image>
          </p>

          <p>
            <m>k(0.5) \approx</m>
          </p>

          <p>
            <m>k(1) \approx</m>
          </p>

          <p>
            <m>k(1.5) \approx</m>
          </p>

          <p>
            <m>k(2) \approx</m>
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            Find the specific solution, <m>k(x)</m>, using the initial condition <m>k(0) = -1</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
        <p>
            <m>L(x)= k(0) +k'(0)x = -1 + x</m>. So
            <m>k(0.5) \approx L(0.5) = -0.5</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
            We organize the computations in Euler's method
            as a table:
            <tabular>
                <row header="yes" bottom="major">
                    <cell><m>x_i</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>k_i</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\frac{dk}{dx}</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\Delta k</m></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell><m>0</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>-1</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>1</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>0.5</m></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell><m>0.5</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>-0.5</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>e^{-.5}+ 0.5^3</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>0.366</m></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell><m>1</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>-0.134</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>e^{-1} + 1</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>0.684</m></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell><m>1.5</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>0.55</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>e^{-1.5}+ 1.5^3</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>1.799</m></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell><m>2</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>2.349</m></cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                </row>
            </tabular>
        </p>
        <p>
            <m>k(x) = -e^{-x}+ 0.25x^4 + C</m>. Using <m>k(0)=-1</m>, we find <m>C= 0.</m>
        </p>
    </solution>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>

    <p>
      Evaluate the following integrals using substitution.
    </p>

    <p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>\displaystyle \int (\cos(x))^{5}\sin(x) dx</m>
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            <m>\displaystyle \int_{2}^{5} \frac{1}{x\ln(x)}dx</m>
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>

    </statement>
    <solution>
        <p>
            <ol>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Choosing <m>u = \cos(x)</m> and substituting, you should obtain 
                        <me>
                            \int -u^5 \quad du = -1/6u^6 + C = -1/6(\cos(x))^6 + C
                        </me>
                        
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Choosing <m>u=\ln(x)</m> and substituting, you should obtain
                        <me>
                            \int_{\ln(x)}^{\ln(5)} \frac{1}{u}du = \ln(\ln(5)) -\ln(\ln(2))
                        </me>
                        
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ol>
        </p>
    </solution>
</exercise>

<exercise>
    <statement>
    <p>
      The velocity graph of a car accelerating from rest to a speed of <m>120</m> km/h over a period of <m>30</m> seconds is shown.
    </p>

    <p>
      <image source="velocitygraph.png">
        <shortdescription>Graph of velocity measured in km/h, versus time measured in seconds.</shortdescription>
      </image>

      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            Estimate the distance traveled during this period using <m>R_{6}</m> and <m>L_{6}</m>.
            Sketch the rectangles used for your estimates on the graph.
            <em>Be careful with units!</em>
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            Estimate the average velocity of the car between <m>0</m> and <m>30</m> seconds.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                See an instructor to verify the sketch of your Riemann sums. After adjusting for units, you should compute
                <me>
                    R_6 \approx 0.7972 \text{ km}
                </me>
                and
                <me>
                    L_6 \approx 0.6306 \text{ km}
                </me>
                
                
            </p>
            <p>
                <m>v_{\text{avg}[0,30]}= \frac{1}{30} \int_0^{30}v(t) \quad dt \approx \frac{1}{30} R_6 = 0.0266 \text{ km/s} = 95.664 \text{ km/hr}</m>
            </p>
        </solution>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>

    <p>
      A particle moves in a straight line and has acceleration given by <m>a(t) = 6t + 4</m> cm/<m>\mathrm{s}^{2}</m>.
      Its initial velocity is <m>v(0) = -6</m> cm/s and its initial displacement is <m>s(0) = 9</m> cm.
      Find its position function <m>s(t).</m>
    </p>

  </statement>
  <solution>
    <p>
        <m>a(t) = 6t + 4</m>
    </p>
    <p>
        <m>v(t) = 3t^2+ 4t - 6</m>
    </p>
    <p>
        <m>s(t) = t^3+ 2t^2 - 6t+9</m>
    </p>
  </solution>
  </exercise>

<exercise>
 <statement>
    <p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            Use the graph of <m>F'(x)</m> and the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus to complete the table of <m>F(x)</m> below.
            Be sure to show your work on how you computed values of the table.
            <image source="FTC.png">
                <shortdescription>Graph of F prime which is constant 2 from x=0 to x=2, decreases linearly
                    with slope -1 until x=4, is constant 0 until x=6, then decreases linearly with slope -0.5.
                </shortdescription>
            </image>
            
          </p>

          <p>
            <tabular>
              <col halign="center" right="minor"> </col> <col halign="center"> </col>
              <row header="yes" bottom="major">
                <cell> <m>x</m> </cell>
                <cell> <m>F(x)</m> </cell>
              </row>

              <row>
                <cell> </cell>
              </row>

              <row>
                <cell> <m>0</m> </cell>
                <cell> <m>5</m> </cell>
              </row>

              <row>
                <cell> </cell>
              </row>

              <row>
                <cell> <m>2</m> </cell>
                <cell> </cell>
              </row>

              <row>
                <cell> </cell>
              </row>

              <row>
                <cell> <m>4</m> </cell>
                <cell> </cell>
              </row>

              <row>
                <cell> </cell>
              </row>

              <row>
                <cell> <m>6</m> </cell>
                <cell> </cell>
              </row>

              <row>
                <cell> </cell>
              </row>

              <row>
                <cell> <m>10</m> </cell>
                <cell> </cell>
              </row>
            </tabular>
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            <ul>
              <li>
                <p>
                  From the information above, we cannot easily compute the average value of
                </p>

                <p>
                  <m>\underset{F(x)/F'(x)}{\underline{\hspace{2.0cm}}}</m> on <m>[0,10]</m> because <em> </em>
                </p>

                <p>
                  <em> </em>.
                </p>
              </li>

              <li>
                <p>
                  From the information above, we can compute the average value of <m>\underset{F(x)/F'(x)}{\underline{\hspace{3cm}}}</m> on <m>[0,10]</m>, and that average value is <em> </em>.
                </p>

                <p>
                  Show how you computed the average here:
                </p>
              </li>
            </ul>
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            If the units of <m>F'(x)</m> are liters per second, what are the units of <m>\int_{0}^{10}F'(x) dx</m>?
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>

</statement>
<solution>
    <p>
        <tabular>
              <col halign="center" right="minor"> </col> <col halign="center"> </col>
              <row header="yes" bottom="major">
                <cell> <m>x</m> </cell>
                <cell> <m>F(x)</m> </cell>
              </row>

              <row>
                <cell> </cell>
              </row>

              <row>
                <cell> <m>0</m> </cell>
                <cell> <m>5</m> </cell>
              </row>

              <row>
                <cell> </cell>
              </row>

              <row>
                <cell> <m>2</m> </cell>
                <cell> <m>9</m></cell>
              </row>

              <row>
                <cell> </cell>
              </row>

              <row>
                <cell> <m>4</m> </cell>
                <cell><m>11</m> </cell>
              </row>

              <row>
                <cell> </cell>
              </row>

              <row>
                <cell> <m>6</m> </cell>
                <cell><m>11</m> </cell>
              </row>

              <row>
                <cell> </cell>
              </row>

              <row>
                <cell> <m>10</m> </cell>
                <cell><m>7</m> </cell>
              </row>
            </tabular>
    </p>
    <p>
        We cannot easily compute the value of <m>F(x)</m>, since we only have a table of <m>5</m> values, and cannot compute
        <m>\int_0^{10} F(x) \quad dx</m> exactly. We can compute the average value of <m>F'(x)</m>, which is 
        <m>1/10 \int_0^{10} F'(x) \quad dx = 1/5</m>.
    </p>
</solution>
</exercise>

